# How fast the port runs when nothing is drawn. The measurement behind "about
# ten times real time" -- and a cheap regression test for the pipe fast path.
name: speed
summary: The opening headless at ten times guest speed, rendering nothing.
frames: 10000
pad: 1600:start,1640:a,1800:start,1840:a,2000:start,2040:a,2200:start,2240:a
pad: 2400:start,2440:a,2600:start,2640:a,2800:start,2840:a,3000:start,3040:a
pad: 3200:start,3240:a
pad: 3600:a@150
env: SOA_SPEED=10

shows: the write-gather pipe fast path, where guest stores go straight to the
shows:   GX parser and nothing is rasterised.
shows: the report's [irq] retrace count against the wall time you measure
shows:   yourself: the saved run managed 594 retraces a second, about ten times
shows:   the console's 59.94. No saved log records wall time (PLAN A4), so time
shows:   the command if you want the rate.

evidence: build/boot_speed.log: 19 scripted events, no [gxr] line, 71,287 VI
evidence:   retraces inside its 120-second watchdog, which is the 594 a second
evidence:   quoted in FINDINGS "Speed" and in PLAN's opening table.

note: SOA_SPEED scales the guest clock the host wall clock drives, so every
note:   device completion moves with it and sound cannot keep up. It is for
note:   exploration and throughput, not for anything that has to sound right.
note: The first nine START/A pairs are the standard preamble: START at 1600
note:   dismisses the title, A at 1640 takes New Game, and the eight pairs that
note:   follow walk the first dialogue boxes. From 3600 one A press every 150
note:   frames advances everything after that. Every saved run in build/ that
note:   got past the title begins with exactly these nineteen events.
